  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nToday-Sunday...A cut-off low in the mid levels will retrograde\nwestward across FL peninsula today and stall over the eastern \nGulf Sunday. This pattern will increase the deep moisture over the\narea with PWATs between 1.8-2.0\" today and exceed 2\" areawide on \nSunday. This moisture combined with persistent northeast to east \nflow will support scattered showers and storms over the adjacent \nAtlc overnight/mornings, drifting toward the coast. Coverage is \nthen anticipated to increase over land as daytime heating takes \nhold, particularly into the afternoon hours as a diffuse east \ncoast sea breeze develops and moves inland, with scattered to \nnumerous showers and storms each day. Rain chances are 50% today \n(60% south) and increase 60-70% Sunday. Temperatures aloft are not\nquite as cold today (-7C to -8C at 500mb) so risk of small hail \nis low. The primary storm hazards will include frequent lightning \nstrikes, wind gusts up to 50 mph, and heavy downpours producing \nquick rainfall accumulations of 1-3\". Shower and storm activity \nwill gradually diminish across the peninsula each evening into the\novernight hours, with the potential for continued westward- \nmoving shower and storm development across the local Atlantic \nwaters. Temperatures are forecast to remain seasonable in the \nupper 80s to low 90s each day, with lows in the 70s each night.\n\nMon-Fri...The cut-off upper low will meander over the eastern Gulf \nto start the week then gradually fill, open up and become absorbed \nby a northern stream trough. Copious moisture will remain over the \narea to support high rain/storm chances esp Mon-Wed (60-80%). A \nfrontal boundary is forecast to reach central FL Thu before stalling \nacross south FL Fri. Slightly drier air should reduce rain chances \nback toward normal (50%) late in the week. A few strong storms each \nafternoon/evening will be capable of frequent lightning, gusty \nwinds, and heavy downpours. Temperatures are forecast to be at or \nslightly above normal in the upper 80s/near 90 with humid conditions \npushing peak heat indices above 100F once again in some spots next \nweek.",
